@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# Curated literal user input — development contract
+
+This extension is development-only. It is not enabled in a production Gateway,
+not upstream compatibility inferred from protocol v4, and not a sandbox for model
+semantics. Adoption requires independent review and an exact derived source/build
+pin; unsupported clients must fail closed, never drop the mode or request admin.
+
+## Admission
+
+`chat.send` accepts optional `inputMode: "literal"` on the existing authenticated
+write route for an existing native session. Create the authorized session first: the
+legacy asynchronous first-send session creation path has no pre-ACK pending-input
+custody owner, so literal first-send creation fails closed rather than weakening
+its durable contract. This restriction does not change absent-mode sends. This waives native text-command interpretation; it does not grant
+admin, system provenance, a profile, an origin, an entitlement, or session access.
+The existing legacy suppression/provenance/admin predicate is unchanged. Literal
+input cannot be combined with those fields, goal/continuation options, reconnect
+resume or trusted internal input. Browser tool bindings remain browser-only.
+Pairing, transport scopes, current session authority, CAS, route and permission
+guards stay in their existing owners. A supplied literal sessionId must match an
+existing destination even without CAS; absent-mode requests retain the older
+sessionId-as-creation-seed behavior when CAS is absent. This closes a generic-client
+SID ambiguity found by a new negative handler test without changing legacy sends. Literal `/stop` is a user message;
+`chat.abort` remains a separately authorized operation. Literal input can still
+cause the model to request tools already authorized for the ordinary caller.
+
+## Versioned durable custody
+
+Every new Gateway user source carries reserved `commandInterpretation` metadata:
+`normal-v1`, `literal-v1`, or `suppressed-v1`, together with `chatRequestIdentity`.
+These are parsing facts, never InputProvenance. Canonical/runtime write hooks
+cannot forge or remove them. Existing literal suppression on the inbound context
+remains in force; the browser restart-safe eligibility is not expanded.
+
+Pending-input hashes independently bind version, mode and input identity, before
+the existing complete request digest. The same hash flows to opt-in terminal
+completion receipts. Single versioned sources now retain consumed receipts just
+as collected sources already did. Thus retiring execution custody, expiry of RAM
+responses, a closed DB or a new process cannot erase parsing identity. The source
+is not dispatched again merely because its terminal response cache expired.
+Retained consumed rows follow the existing session deletion/repair owners.
+
+Before command shortcuts, response replay or new admission, mode/identity must
+match in both directions. An unreadable versioned custody record, unknown version,
+partial required metadata or a known literal retry with no proof is a conflict.
+Matching in-memory identities and browser-only restart fingerprints remain fast
+paths (literal is not eligible for the latter). Goal receipts retain their owner,
+but a goal cannot adopt a literal source ID. A custody digest may establish parsing
+identity after a completion intentionally removed source bytes; full request
+matching remains the stage/receipt owner's job, not an invented ACK.
+
+Early admission has its own bounded responsibility in the session accessor:
+`session_chat_input_admissions` binds the exact store/key, original SID (or the
+legacy normal first-send absence), operation ID, parsing version/identity and
+attempt before publishing the abortable RAM reservation. It stores no message
+bytes and grants no execution or cancellation authority. The additive feature
+table is installed on writes only; legacy reads do not migrate it.
+
+An early abort commits its terminal timestamp/reason under the exact durable
+attempt and synchronous live-owner check before publishing a marker or reply.
+Persistence failure leaves the RAM reservation intact and publishes no abort
+success. A stale attempt cannot retire another reservation or terminal outcome.
+The admission retains its exact terminal writer if maintenance retires an expired
+pending entry. The terminal fact is first-writer-sticky and is replayed before
+command shortcuts, including absent-mode stop parsing. These compact identity
+rows have no independent cache TTL/capacity pruning: neither five-minute response
+retirement nor one-hour marker retirement can turn a versioned operation into
+legacy no-evidence input. An original non-null SID never transfers to another
+incarnation. This is conservative idempotency evidence, not an operation adoption
+or new recovery mechanism.
+
+Unconsumed interrupted work still uses the original native recovery/admission
+rules. This change does not promise re-execution of unknown outcomes or enable a
+new browser/adapter restart path. An unsupported recovery path remains stopped.
+
+## Explicit pre-extension behavior
+
+Old records without parsing evidence are normal-only: ordinary old retries keep
+their existing behavior; literal retries cannot upgrade them. Only the normal
+version may reconstruct an old unversioned digest for a consumed source (including
+the shipped client-transport variation). Changed bytes, literal mode and new
+unknown versions cannot use that compatibility branch. Pre-extension records are
+not rewritten on read. No new global schema/readiness gate is imposed.
+
+## Development proof and adoption gates
+
+`literal-review.vitest.config.ts` selects native auth, current-authority, session,
+request, command routing, source/collector/steer, retry, cancellation and lifecycle
+regressions plus this extension's tests. The external task runner denies network;
+the provider-free Gateway shutdown/restart fixture is run separately with only
+loopback sockets allowed and external networking still denied. The two original participant-retarget failures were runner errors:
+thread-pool workers are not SQLite's host owner. The exact unmodified baseline
+passes under the fork pool; no host guard, production participant code or assertion
+was weakened. Retained external receipts document the failing and passing runs.
+
+Fresh-process tests use task-owned SQLite only, not providers or accounts. Signed
+source and full upstream-to-derived diff digests are separate external review
+artifacts. Shipping still requires sealed dependency/archive provenance, confined
+paired WebSocket and host-tool/memory acceptance, native shutdown/restart acceptance,
+profile/journal migration decisions and parent review before any adapter repin.
